Router:
- routes traffic between IP subnets
- routers inside of switches are sometimes called "layer 3 switches"
- Layer 2 = switch
- Layer 3 = router
- Connect LAN, WAN, copper, fiber

Switch:
- bridging done in hardware
- Application-specific integrated circuit (ASIC)
- OSI layer 2 device - forwards traffic based on data link address
- Power over Ethernet (PoE)
- Multilayer switch (layer 3) if routing functionality included

Firewall:
- filter traffic by port number or application
- encrypt traffic, VPN between sites
- can be used as a router (layer 3)
- NAT

Load Balancer:
- TCP offload
- SSL offload (provides encryption/decryption instead of servers)
- cache data
- Prioritize (QoS)
- Content switching

Proxies:
- sits between users and external network

Network Attached Storage (NAS):
- file level access
- pull entire file into memory, change file data, then write entire file back

Storage Area Network (SAN):
- block level access
- can change just what is changed for file

Access Point:
- layer two (data link)
- ethernet cable

Wireless LAN controllers:
- centralized management of access points
- deploy new access points
- monitoring APs
- configure and deploy changes to all sites